* START-HISTORY:
* 19 Oct 26 dm  The verifier's finding F1: the files are made under umask 077 (the modes are 0700 and 0600 now, set by the kernel as well), and the umask is put back.
* 19 Oct 26 dm  New, S.67 stage 1.  Called by the SYNC.MASTERS verb and by upgrade_masters (the installer).  The journal is made BEFORE the files
*               (the first kernel(K$MASTERS, 'init')), because a system that has master files and no journal raises the stale flag, and the files'
*               modes are set by a second 'init' after they are made.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    call !masters_setup(report)
*
* REPORT<1> is 0 when the master files and the journal are in place, else a status (1 the SDSYS VOC cannot be opened, 2 a file cannot be made, 3 the
* kernel's set-up failed); <2> says what was wrong or, when all is well, what the kernel did (journal=created|exists modes=n); <3> is 1 when
* anything was made by this call.
*
* The three master files (voc_master, dict_master, master_state) are hashed and case-insensitive like every file this system makes, each with a
* dictionary holding the default @id record, and each with an F entry in the SDSYS VOC (and in NO other VOC: a new account is made from NEWVOC, which
* has none).  The files are made by path, so the account the administrator happens to be in does not matter.  The modes (0700 directories, 0600
* files: the sdsys user alone) and the journal ($master.journal, mode 0620, created here and never by a session) are set by the kernel
* (kernel(K$MASTERS, 'init')).  It is safe to run again: whatever is there is left alone, and its modes are set again.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine masters_setup(report)
$catalog !masters_setup

$include int$keys.h
$include keys.h

   report = ''
   made = 0
   um = 0
   ans = ''
   nm = ''
   dp = ''

   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'voc' to sv else
      report = 1 : @fm : 'the SDSYS VOC cannot be opened' : @fm : 0
      return
   end

*  The journal first: from here on a change to a VOC or a dictionary is written to it, and while the files below are being made nothing is lost (a system that
*  has master files and no journal raises the stale flag, which this avoids).  The files' modes are set by the second call.
   ans = kernel(K$MASTERS, 'init')
   if ans<1> # 0 then
      close sv
      report = 3 : @fm : 'the kernel refused its set-up (status ' : ans<1> : ')' : @fm : 0
      return
   end
   if index(ans<2>, 'journal=created', 1) then made = 1

*  The files are made under a umask of 077 (63): the sdsys user alone may open them from the moment they exist, whatever this session's umask and the group of the
*  directory they are made in (the kernel sets the modes again below; this closes the time before it does).  Put back on every way out.
   um = umask(63)
   for each nm in 'voc_master' : @fm : 'dict_master' : @fm : 'master_state'
      dp = @sdsys : @ds : nm
      if not(ospath(dp, OS$EXISTS)) then
         create.file dp dynamic on error
            close sv
            if umask(um) then null
            report = 2 : @fm : 'cannot make ' : nm : @fm : made
            return
         end
         made = 1
      end
      if not(ospath(dp : '.dic', OS$EXISTS)) then
         create.file dp : '.dic' dynamic on error
            close sv
            if umask(um) then null
            report = 2 : @fm : 'cannot make ' : nm : '.dic' : @fm : made
            return
         end
         made = 1
         openpath dp : '.dic' to df then
            write 'D' : @fm : '0' : @fm : '' : @fm : nm : @fm : '30L' : @fm : 'S' to df, '@id'
            close df
         end
      end
      read x from sv, nm else
         write 'F' : @fm : nm : @fm : nm : '.dic' to sv, nm
         made = 1
      end
   next nm
   close sv
   if umask(um) then null

   ans = kernel(K$MASTERS, 'init')        ;* again, for the modes of the files that are there now
   if ans<1> # 0 then
      report = 3 : @fm : 'the kernel refused its set-up (status ' : ans<1> : ')' : @fm : made
      return
   end
   report = 0 : @fm : ans<2> : @fm : made
   return
end

* END-CODE
